    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "N-terminal (Nt-) acetylation is a common cotranslational protein modification found in over 80% of human proteins. A recent study identified a variant (c.128 A > C; p.Tyr43Ser) of N-alpha acetyltransferase 10 (NAA10), the gene that encodes the catalytic subunit of the primary human Nt-acetylation catalyst, as the cause of a novel syndrome characterized by cardiac dysfunction and other severe clinical features. Variants of NAA10 have also been associated with Ogden syndrome, Lenz microphthalmia syndrome, and other serious disorders, but the mechanisms that support the severe and complex phenotypes observed in patients with NAA10-associated diseases have remained unclear. Human induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs) provide a unique platform for studying genetic human diseases because they can be created from the somatic cells of affected patients and, consequently, contain all of the genetic components associated with the disease phenotype. This study shows that when iPSCs were reprogrammed from the peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of patients carrying the Tyr43Ser NAA10 variant and were differentiated into cardiomyocytes (CMs), the iPSC-CMs displayed aberrations in calcium handling and action potential measurements (i.e., arrhythmia and prolonged QT intervals) that were consistent with the patients’ cardiac symptoms. Furthermore, the abnormalities could be rescued by using CRISPR/Cas9 and ssODN-mediated gene-editing technology to restore the normal NAA10 sequence, which confirmed that the Tyr43Ser mutation is necessary for the disease phenotype. These observations establish iPSC-CMs as a powerful tool for modeling the disease. Additional investigations with this disease model will improve patient diagnosis and treatment, enable screening for drugs that reverse the disease phenotype, and advance our understanding of the mechanisms underlying related cardiomyopathies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
